(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-2-integumentary-system-quiz"]={"title":"The Integumentary System — Anatomy Exam 2","category":"Anatomy","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"What structures make up the integumentary system?","choices":["Only skin and hair, without any glandular or nail components","Skin, hair, nails, sweat glands, oil glands, mammary glands, and enamel of teeth","Only sweat glands and oil glands, excluding skin, hair, and nails entirely","Skin and the skeletal system, sharing a common connective tissue origin"],"answer":1,"exp":"The integumentary system is composed of skin, hair, nails, sweat glands, oil glands, mammary glands, and enamel of teeth. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"What are the five functions of the skin listed in the material?","choices":["Only heat regulation, maintained through sweat glands and superficial blood vessels","Only protection and sensation, via the epidermis and dermal nerve endings","Protection, containment, heat regulation, sensation, and synthesis/storage of vitamin D","Digestion and absorption of nutrients, aided by specialized epidermal enzymes"],"answer":2,"exp":"The skin's functions are protection (from environmental effects), containment (of tissues/organs, preventing dehydration), heat regulation (via sweat glands/blood vessels/fat), sensation (via nerve endings), and synthesis/storage of vitamin D. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"What are the two main layers of the skin, and what characterizes each?","choices":["Epidermis (superficial cellular layer, tough protective outer surface) and dermis (basal regenerative and pigmented connective tissue layer)","Epidermis (deep connective tissue layer) and dermis (superficial cellular layer), reversed from their actual positions","Subcutaneous tissue and deep fascia, forming the two main structural layers described in this material","Only one layer: the epidermis, since the dermis is classified as an entirely separate connective tissue organ"],"answer":0,"exp":"The skin consists of the epidermis, a superficial cellular layer creating a tough protective outer surface, and the dermis, a basal (deep) regenerative and pigmented connective tissue layer. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"What is the epidermis composed of, and is it vascular?","choices":["A keratinized simple (single-layer) epithelium; highly vascular throughout its thickness","A keratinized stratified epithelium with a tough outer surface of keratin; avascular, nourished by vessels in the underlying dermis","Smooth muscle tissue; avascular, relying on diffusion from the underlying dermis","A single layer of connective tissue; highly vascular, supplying nutrients directly"],"answer":1,"exp":"The epidermis is a keratinized stratified (layered) epithelium with a tough outer surface composed of keratin; it is avascular and nourished by vessels in the underlying dermis. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"How often is the epidermis renewed, per the material?","choices":["Every 24 hours","Never — epidermal cells do not turn over","Every 5 years","Every 25-45 days"],"answer":3,"exp":"The outer layer of the epidermis is continuously shed or rubbed away, with replacement of new cells from the basal layer, renewing the entire epidermis of the body every 25-45 days. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"What are the five layers of the epidermis, from top (superficial) to bottom (deep)?","choices":["Stratum corneum, stratum basale, stratum granulosum, stratum lucidum, stratum spinosum","Only three layers exist: corneum, spinosum, and basale","Stratum basale, stratum spinosum, stratum granulosum, stratum lucidum, stratum corneum (reversed order is correct top to bottom)","Stratum corneum, stratum lucidum, stratum granulosum, stratum spinosum, stratum germinativum/basale"],"answer":3,"exp":"The five layers of the epidermis, from top (superficial) to bottom (deep), are the stratum corneum, stratum lucidum, stratum granulosum, stratum spinosum, and stratum germinativum/basale. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What is the stratum germinativum/basale, and what cell types does it contain?","choices":["A layer found only in the thick skin of the palms and soles, containing several rows of dead, clear cells lacking nuclei","A single layer of stem cells firmly attached to the basal lamina, composed of basal cells and melanocytes (ratio 4:1 to 20:1), refurbishing keratinocytes","The most superficial epidermal layer, containing only dead keratin and no living or dividing cells of any kind","A layer of dead, flattened cells with no nucleus, forming the outermost protective barrier against water loss"],"answer":1,"exp":"The stratum germinativum/basale is a single layer of stem cells firmly attached to the basal lamina, separating the dermis from the epidermis; it is composed of basal cells and melanocytes in a ratio of 4:1 to 20:1, and it refurbishes keratinocytes (also containing Merkel cells). — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"What do Merkel cells in the stratum basale do?","choices":["Produce lamellar granules","Present antigens to T cells","Stimulate sensory nerve endings","Produce melanin, coloring nearby keratinocytes"],"answer":2,"exp":"Merkel cells, found in the stratum basale, stimulate sensory nerve endings. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"What is the stratum spinosum, and what cell type initiates the immune response there?","choices":["Located above the stratum spinosum, only a few cells thick, containing mostly melanocytes connected by desmosomes; Merkel cells (about 6%) initiate the immune response","Located above the stratum lucidum, several cells thick, containing mostly corneocytes connected by desmosomes; dendritic cells (about 6%) initiate the immune response","Located above the stratum corneum, a single cell thick, containing mostly melanocytes connected by tight junctions; mast cells (about 10%) initiate the immune response","Located above the stratum basale, several cells thick, containing mostly keratinocytes connected by tonofibrils; Langerhans cells (about 6%) initiate the immune response"],"answer":3,"exp":"The stratum spinosum is located above the stratum basale, several cells thick, containing mostly keratinocytes connected to each other by tonofibrils, with some melanocytes and Langerhans cells (about 6%) that initiate the immune response. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What is produced in the stratum granulosum, and what effect does keratohyalin have?","choices":["Keratohyalin and keratin are produced; keratohyalin coats cells with a lipid-rich substance making the layer water-resistant and causing the top layer of cells to die","Only lipids are produced; lamellar bodies coat cells with a protein-rich substance making the layer flexible and causing the bottom layer of cells to die","Filaggrin and collagen are produced; filaggrin coats cells with a keratin-rich substance making the layer elastic and causing the entire layer to regenerate","Sebum and keratin are produced; sebum coats cells with a water-rich substance making the layer permeable and causing the middle layer of cells to proliferate"],"answer":0,"exp":"The stratum granulosum, located above the stratum spinosum, is where keratohyalin and keratin are produced; keratohyalin granules coat cells with a lipid-rich substance, making the layer water-resistant and causing the top layer of cells to die (this layer also forms calluses). — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Where is the stratum lucidum found, and what are its cells like?","choices":["Found only in the axillae and groin folds, covering the stratum corneum; cells retain a nucleus, are rounded and loosely packed, filled with lipid droplets oriented perpendicular to the surface","Found throughout thin skin of the face and trunk, covering the stratum spinosum; cells have a single nucleus, are cuboidal and loosely packed, filled with melanin granules scattered randomly","Found in thick skin of the palms and soles, covering the stratum granulosum; cells have no nucleus, are flattened and densely packed, filled with keratin filaments oriented parallel to the surface","Found only in the scalp and eyebrows, covering the stratum basale; cells have multiple nuclei, are columnar and sparsely packed, filled with tonofibrils oriented vertically to the surface"],"answer":2,"exp":"The stratum lucidum is found in thick skin of the palms and soles, covering the stratum granulosum; its cells have no nucleus, are flattened and densely packed, and are filled with keratin filaments oriented parallel to the surface. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What characterizes the stratum corneum, and how long does it take keratinocytes to travel there and be shed?","choices":["Most superficial layer, 15-30 layers of flattened keratinized cells, water-resistant but not waterproof; keratinocytes take 15-30 days to arrive and are shed in about 2 weeks","The deepest epidermal layer, a single cell thick, fully waterproof and impermeable; keratinocytes take 5-7 days to arrive and are never shed throughout life","A layer found only over the anterior eye surface, 3-5 layers of nucleated cells, fully permeable to water; keratinocytes take 1-2 days to arrive and are shed within hours","A layer that never sheds cells throughout life, 40-50 layers of cuboidal cells, entirely waterproof; keratinocytes take 60-90 days to arrive and accumulate permanently"],"answer":0,"exp":"The stratum corneum is the most superficial layer of the skin, consisting of 15-30 layers of flattened, keratinized cells; it is relatively dry, water-resistant but not waterproof (allowing perspiration), with keratinocytes taking 15-30 days to travel from the basal layer to this layer and being shed in about 2 weeks. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"What forms epidermal ridges, and what is their function?","choices":["Formed by the stratum basale, with dermal papillae extending into the epidermis; increases contact area between dermis and epidermis, forms skin contours (including fingerprints), and ensures a secure grip","Formed by the stratum lucidum, with epidermal pegs extending into the dermis; decreases contact area between dermis and epidermis, forms scar tissue patterns, and increases the risk of blistering","Formed by the stratum corneum, with keratin plugs extending into the dermis; has no effect on contact area between layers, forms only skin pigmentation patterns, and plays no role in grip","Formed by melanocytes, with pigment granules extending into the epidermis; increases only skin darkness in sun-exposed areas, forms freckles and moles, and has no role in grip or fingerprints"],"answer":0,"exp":"Epidermal ridges are formed by the stratum basale, with dermal papillae extending into the epidermis from the dermis; they increase the contact area between the dermis and epidermis, form the contours of the skin (including fingerprints), do not change throughout life, and ensure a secure grip. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What factors mainly determine skin color, per the material?","choices":["Only the density of hair follicles and the distribution of sweat glands across the body surface","Only the number of active sebaceous glands and the thickness of the subcutaneous fat layer","Dermal blood supply, thickness of the stratum corneum, and variable quantity of carotene and melanin","Only the pattern of dermal papillae and the density of cutaneous sensory nerve endings"],"answer":2,"exp":"Skin color is mainly due to dermal blood supply, thickness of the stratum corneum, and the variable quantity of carotene and melanin (genetically controlled). — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What is the difference between carotene and melanin?","choices":["Carotene is a blue-black pigment converted to vitamin D, found in melanocytes; melanin is produced/stored in keratinocytes, forms in lysosomes, and prevents bacterial skin infection","Carotene is an orange-yellow pigment converted to vitamin A, found in keratinocytes; melanin is produced/stored in melanocytes, forms in melanosomes, and prevents UV skin damage","Carotene is a colorless compound converted to vitamin C, found in fibroblasts; melanin is produced/stored in Langerhans cells, forms in mitochondria, and increases skin water loss","Carotene is a red-brown pigment converted to vitamin K, found in Merkel cells; melanin is produced/stored in keratinocytes, forms in the Golgi apparatus, and regulates body temperature"],"answer":1,"exp":"Carotene is an orange-yellow pigment that converts to vitamin A and is found in keratinocytes, while melanin is produced and stored in melanocytes (forming in melanosomes, transferred into keratinocytes, and eventually destroyed by lysosomes), preventing skin damage from UV light. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What is the dermis formed by, and what determines the tension (cleavage) lines and wrinkles in the skin?","choices":["A thin layer of simple squamous epithelium; the density of sweat glands determines tension lines and wrinkles","A loose layer of adipose and areolar tissue; the thickness of the subcutaneous fat determines tension lines and wrinkles","A dense layer of interlacing collagen and elastic fibers; the direction of the collagen fibers determines tension lines and wrinkles","A single layer of stratified keratinized cells; the amount of melanin pigment determines tension lines and wrinkles"],"answer":2,"exp":"The dermis is formed by a dense layer of interlacing collagen and elastic fibers, which provide skin tone and account for the skin's strength and toughness; the direction of the collagen fibers determines the tension lines (cleavage lines) and wrinkles in the skin. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"What happens when the arrector pili muscles in the dermis contract?","choices":["Flattens the hair follicle and dilates sweat glands, helping them secrete watery fluid onto the skin","Relaxes the hair shaft and constricts blood vessels, helping reduce blood flow to the dermis","Loosens the hair root and stimulates melanocytes, helping them release pigment into the epidermis","Erects the hair (causing goose bumps) and compresses sebaceous glands, helping them secrete oily product onto the skin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Contraction of the arrector pili muscles erects the hair, causing goose bumps, and compresses the associated sebaceous glands, helping them secrete oily product onto the skin. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"What is the difference between the papillary layer and reticular layer of the dermis?","choices":["Papillary layer is dense, irregular connective tissue surrounding blood vessels and nerves with interwoven collagen bundles; reticular layer is loose connective tissue containing only capillaries, lacking any nerve or gland association within the dermis","Papillary layer is composed of dense irregular connective tissue surrounding hair follicles and glands; reticular layer is loose areolar tissue containing capillaries and axons, positioned superficial to the papillary layer near the epidermis","Papillary layer is loose connective tissue containing capillaries and axons; reticular layer is dense, irregular connective tissue surrounding blood vessels/hair follicles/nerves/glands with interwoven collagen bundles extending into the subcutaneous layer","The dermis has only one undifferentiated connective tissue layer, containing scattered capillaries and axons but no distinction between superficial and deep regions, and no association with hair follicles, nerves, or glands whatsoever"],"answer":2,"exp":"The papillary layer is loose connective tissue containing capillaries and axons, while the reticular layer is dense, irregular connective tissue surrounding blood vessels, hair follicles, nerves, and glands, with interwoven collagen fiber bundles extending deep into the subcutaneous layer. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"What is the subcutaneous tissue (superficial fascia) composed of, and what does it contain?","choices":["Keratinized epithelial cells identical to the epidermis, located superficial to the dermis; contains melanocytes, Langerhans cells, and Merkel cells, providing the body's primary barrier to water loss","Loose connective tissue and fat, located between the dermis and deep fascia; contains the deepest parts of sweat glands, blood/lymphatic vessels, and cutaneous nerves, providing most of the body's fat storage","Only epithelial cells with no connective tissue component, located between the epidermis and dermis; contains only sensory nerve endings, providing the body's sense of touch and pressure","Dense, organized connective tissue without fat, located deep to the deep fascia; contains tendons, ligaments, and periosteum, providing structural support and muscle attachment sites"],"answer":1,"exp":"The subcutaneous tissue (superficial fascia) is composed of loose connective tissue and fat, located between the dermis and underlying deep fascia; it contains the deepest parts of the sweat glands, blood and lymphatic vessels, and cutaneous nerves, and provides most of the body's fat storage (with thickness varying by nutritional state). — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"What are skin ligaments, and what do they determine about skin mobility?","choices":["Numerous small fibrous bands extending through the subcutaneous tissue, attaching the dermis to the deep fascia; skin mobility over deep structures depends on their length and density","A single thick muscular band extending through the epidermis, attaching the stratum corneum to the stratum basale; skin mobility over deep structures depends on hydration and elasticity","Bundles of contractile smooth muscle fibers extending through the dermis, producing voluntary movement of the skin; skin mobility over deep structures depends on nervous stimulation frequency","A network of elastic sheets found only in the epidermis, unrelated to the subcutaneous tissue; skin mobility over deep structures depends entirely on stratum corneum thickness"],"answer":0,"exp":"Skin ligaments consist of numerous small fibrous bands that extend through the subcutaneous tissue and attach the deep surface of the dermis to the underlying deep fascia; the mobility of the skin over deep structures depends on the length and density of these ligaments. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"What is deep fascia, and what are its extensions called when they invest deeper structures versus divide muscles into compartments?","choices":["The outermost keratinized layer of the epidermis, enveloping only the palms and soles; dermal papillae invest deeper structures, and rete ridges divide the epidermis into segments","A dense, organized connective tissue layer without fat, enveloping most of the body deep to skin/subcutaneous tissue; investing fascia invests deeper structures, and intermuscular septa divide muscles into compartments","A loose, fat-containing layer found only in the limbs, absent from the trunk and head; superficial fascia invests deeper structures, and skin ligaments divide muscles into compartments","A thin, vascular connective tissue layer identical to subcutaneous tissue, enveloping only joints; retinacula invest deeper structures, and bursae divide muscles into compartments"],"answer":1,"exp":"Deep fascia is a dense, organized connective tissue layer without fat, enveloping most of the body deep to the skin and subcutaneous tissue; its extensions include investing fascia (investing deeper structures like individual muscles and neurovascular bundles) and intermuscular septa (dividing muscles into groups or compartments, important for compartment syndrome). — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"What are retinacula and bursae, as structures formed by deep fascia?","choices":["Retinacula hold tendons in place during joint movement; bursae prevent friction and enable structures to move freely over one another","Retinacula secrete synovial fluid into joint capsules; bursae anchor ligaments to the periosteum of adjacent bones","Retinacula transmit nerve signals across joint spaces; bursae store fat deposits around moving tendons","Retinacula prevent friction between adjacent tendons during movement; bursae anchor tendons firmly in place across joints"],"answer":0,"exp":"Deep fascia also forms retinacula, which hold tendons in place during movement of joints, and bursae, which prevent friction and enable structures to move freely over one another. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"What are fascial planes, and why are they important surgically?","choices":["The same fluid-filled sacs as bursae, important in preventing friction between tendons and bones during joint movement","Potential spaces between adjacent fascias or fascia-lined structures, important in surgical procedures to separate structures and create access to deeper structures","Actual, pre-existing fluid-filled spaces found only in the dermis, important in regulating interstitial fluid pressure beneath the epidermis","Dense bands of collagen fibers connecting muscle to bone, important in transmitting contractile force during movement"],"answer":1,"exp":"Fascial planes are potential spaces between adjacent fascias or fascia-lined structures; these planes are important in surgical procedures to separate structures, creating spaces that allow access to deeper structures. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"How does the skin provide containment, per the material?","choices":["Contains the tissues, organs, and vital substances of the body, preventing dehydration","By actively absorbing external fluids and electrolytes from the environment into the body","Only through the contraction of arrector pili muscles, producing goose bumps in cold weather","Only by producing sweat continuously to cool the body during physical exertion"],"answer":0,"exp":"The skin's containment function involves containing the tissues, organs, and vital substances of the body, preventing dehydration. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"How does the skin achieve heat regulation, per the material?","choices":["Only through variation in stratum corneum thickness","Through sweat glands, blood vessels, and fat deposits","Only through variation in melanin production levels","Only through changes in nail bed blood flow rate"],"answer":1,"exp":"The skin achieves heat regulation through sweat glands, blood vessels, and fat deposits. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"How does the skin provide sensation, and what example is given?","choices":["By way of superficial nerves and their sensory endings, e.g. pain","By way of arrector pili muscle contraction, e.g. touch","By way of melanocytes detecting ultraviolet light","By way of the nail bed's blood vessels and their sensation only"],"answer":0,"exp":"The skin provides sensation by way of superficial nerves and their sensory endings, with pain given as an example. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"What does the skin synthesize and store, per its listed functions?","choices":["Insulin","Vitamin C","Vitamin D","Melatonin"],"answer":2,"exp":"The skin synthesizes and stores vitamin D. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"What happens to melanin after it is transferred into keratinocytes?","choices":["It is transported back into melanocytes","It converts into keratin","It is eventually destroyed by lysosomes","It permanently remains unchanged for the life of the cell"],"answer":2,"exp":"Melanin, after being produced in melanosomes and transferred into keratinocytes, is eventually destroyed by lysosomes. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What structures connect keratinocytes to each other within the stratum spinosum?","choices":["Melanosomes","Keratohyalin granules","Tonofibrils","Hemidesmosomes to the basal lamina only"],"answer":2,"exp":"Keratinocytes within the stratum spinosum are connected to other keratinocytes by tonofibrils. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"How does the skin provide protection, per the material?","choices":["Only by regulating body temperature via evaporative cooling mechanisms","Only through sensory nerve endings that detect pressure and touch","Protects the body from environmental effects, such as abrasions and harmful substances","Only by producing vitamin D upon direct sun exposure to the epidermis and dermis"],"answer":2,"exp":"The skin's protection function guards the body from environmental effects, such as abrasions and harmful substances. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 4"}]};
